desc:ChannelView Modulators
author: Tim Shadgett
noindex: true

/*
  TS_CV_Modulators.jsfx -- ChannelView's macros and modulators for one track.
  Set up and shown by ChannelView's Macros and Modulators panels; every
  setting here is a parameter, so each can have an automation lane.

  Controls take their value from this plugin through REAPER's own
  parameter links (Parameter modulation > Link from MIDI or FX parameter).
  Audio and MIDI pass through untouched.

  Parameters (0-based, as REAPER numbers them):
    0-15      Macro 1-16
    16 + 22*(n-1) + k   Modulator n's field k:
       0  Type
       1  Output
       2  Output bipolar
       3  Depth
       4  Smooth
       5  LFO Shape
       6  LFO Sync
       7  LFO Rate (sync)
       8  LFO Rate (Hz)
       9  LFO Phase
      10  Follower Source
      11  Follower Attack
      12  Follower Release
      13  Follower Floor
      14  Follower Ceiling
      15  Follower Gate
          A Follower's MIDI Source (16) 5-7 makes it an envelope the
          audio sets off instead (THE FOLLOWER'S ENVELOPES, below).
      16  MIDI Source: Velocity, Note, CC, Pitch bend, Aftertouch, and
          the envelopes a note sets off -- Envelope, Envelope x velocity
          (its peak the velocity), Duck (the envelope upside down: down
          from 1 on each note, back over the release). An envelope uses
          the Follower's Attack and Release, Floor as its Sustain
          (-80..0 dB is 0..100 %) and Ceiling as its Decay (-60..12 is
          0..2000 ms on a square scale); Gate On makes its decay and
          release exponential, and Hold On (legato) starts it again only
          from no notes held. Low and High note pick the notes it takes.
      17  MIDI Channel
      18  MIDI CC
      19  MIDI Low note
      20  MIDI High note
      21  MIDI Hold
  Output is 0..1 from rest (Depth scales it); Output bipolar swings
  round 0.5 (Depth scales the swing), for controls driven both ways.
    192 + 6*(n-1) + 3*(s-1) + k   Modulator n's source s (of 2), field k:
       0  Source   0 none, 1-16 Macro 1-16, 17-24 Modulator 1-8
       1  Target   which setting it moves (THE MATRIX below), + 100 x the
                   macro's choices when the target is a switch
       2  Amount   a setting's share of its knob's range, -1..1; for a
                   switch, the setting by choice, one decimal digit each

  THE MATRIX. Each modulator's settings can be moved
  by two sources: a macro, or another modulator. Worked out here, at the
  top of every block -- the moved settings are written over the knobs'
  for this block and put back at its end, so the knobs (and their lanes)
  keep their own values. A modulator moved by another uses that one's
  output from the block before, so two can move each other.
  Targets: 1 Depth, 2 Rate (Hz, or the synced rate in whole steps),
  3 Phase, 4 Smooth, 5 Attack, 6 Release, 7 Floor, 8 Ceiling, 9 Low
  note, 10 High note -- each moved within its knob's own scale -- and
  the switches, from a button or dropdown macro only: 11 Shape, 12 Sync,
  13 Follower source, 14 Gate, 15 Hold. A switch's Amount holds, in its
  decimal digits from the lowest, the setting for each of the macro's
  choices (0 its own, 1.. the setting + 1), up to six; the choices count
  is the Target's hundreds.
  A project saved without these loads them at 0: nothing moved, every
  link and lane where it was.

    240       Bypass all: every modulator held at rest (Output 0, Output
              bipolar 0.5), so each control it drives sits where it's set.
              Macros carry on.
    240 + n   Modulator n's bypass, the same for that one alone.
    249       Trigger tracks: modulator n (bit n-1 set) takes its notes
              only from another track's MIDI send, which arrives on MIDI
              bus 8 + n and goes no further than this plugin.
    250       Instance: this plugin's number, given it by ChannelView,
              which talks to it by that number (STEPS AND MSEG, below).

  STEPS AND MSEG (Types 4 and 5). Their settings are in the fields the
  other kinds use; their pattern (a Steps' 32 values and which of them
  are joined to the one before, an MSEG's points) is kept here, with the
  project, and ChannelView reads and writes it through gmem TS_CV_Mod:
     0 a message's number (ChannelView counts them), 1 the Instance it's
     for, 2 the modulator (1-8), 3 what: 1 read the steps, 2 write them,
     3 read the points, 4 write them; 4 the number of the last one done;
     10.. the pattern (steps: 32 values then 32 joins; points: how many,
     then x, y and bend for each). 1000 + 8 x (Instance mod 8192) + n - 1:
     where modulator n is now (the step playing, or 0..1 along the MSEG).
  Steps: Attack the number of steps (2-32), Rate (sync) each step's
  length, Shape the direction (Forward, Reverse, Ping pong, Random),
  Floor the swing (0..-80 is 0..80 %), Gate whether it's bipolar (for
  ChannelView only), Smooth its glide. MIDI out in MIDI Source: 0 none,
  1 CC (MIDI CC its number), 2 notes, on MIDI Channel (Omni is 1): each
  run of joined steps one note, its value across Low..High note on the
  scale and key in Release (scale x 12 + key), Ceiling its gate (-60..12
  is 0..100 %), MIDI CC its velocity; a step at the bottom is a rest.
  Notes only while the transport plays.
  MSEG: Rate (sync) or (Hz) its length, Phase where it starts; MIDI
  Source its trigger: 0 the song position, 1 free running, 2 a MIDI note
  (Channel, Low and High note), 3 the audio going over the Floor (Follower
  Source the track or sidechain); Hold its loop: off, a trigger runs it
  once and it holds at its end.

  THE FOLLOWER'S ENVELOPES. A Follower whose MIDI Source is 5, 6 or 7
  is an Envelope, Envelope x level (its peak how far the loudest of the
  hit went past the Floor, full at the Ceiling) or Duck, set off by its
  audio instead of notes: it starts when the level rises above the
  Floor (its threshold) and is let go when it falls 3 dB under it. The
  level is read fast (1 ms up, 30 ms down) whatever Attack and Release
  say, which are the envelope's. Decay is in MIDI CC (0..127, square to
  2000 ms) and Sustain in Low note (0..127 is 0..100 %); Gate On is the
  exponential curve. Hold On (legato): a rise while it's still letting go
  carries on from where it is; off, every rise starts it from nothing.

  The idea of macros and modulators living in the chain, linked by
  REAPER's parameter modulation, comes from Saxmand's FX Modulator
  Linking (Jesper Ankarfeldt), with thanks.
*/

